In uniform-background grids with sparse colored markers, avoid assuming that all cells between the global extrema in each row or column are recolored. Compare each demo cell-by-cell and distinguish uninterrupted marker-bounded spans from runs interrupted by markers and from background outside the endpoints. Test row-wise and column-wise span hypotheses separately; output may combine both orientations, and marker cells should remain unchanged if the demonstrations show that. Validate the complete predicted output against every demo before applying it to the test.

For programmatic construction, guard rows/columns with no markers before computing minimum and maximum coordinates. Finally assert the number of rows and each row width against expected dimensions; a malformed row must not be submitted.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/sparse-span-fill-and-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/failed-demo-request-loop.md", "content": "# Failed demo-request loop: enforce an evidence checkpoint

A costly ARC instance can go badly even when the assistant correctly recognizes that demos need reconciliation: repeatedly loading guidance is not the same as applying it. In this instance, six heterogeneous demos were received; the assistant made one unsupported recoloring submission, then requested additional demos without comparing exact changed-cell masks or naming live hypotheses that would be discriminated. The final received example was left unused. This is a process failure, not evidence for a particular transformation.

Reusable procedure: after every demo, pause and compare input/output changed locations, note preserved counterexamples, and test candidate rules against all pairs. Before another request, name at least two plausible rules and how a possible demo result would distinguish them and alter the test prediction. If the noisy/heterogeneous pairs do not establish a coherent rule, say so internally and stop issuing generic requests; use the best-supported prediction while budget remains. Following an incorrect submission, rebuild the full hypothesis from evidence rather than returning to requests by default. Preserve exactly one JSON action per evaluator turn.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/failed-demo-request-loop.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/stripe-sequence-validation.md", "content": "# Uniform stripe sequence tasks: validation and revision

For grids made of full-width, single-color rows, collapse each grid to a 1-D sequence of row colors before inferring the transformation. Compare input and output sequences position by position, including leading and trailing background rows and every gap between visible stripes. Check whether output preserves anchor rows, repeats a motif, extends a sequence, or uses a phase determined by the first/last marker. Do not infer a 2-D periodic tiling merely because other tasks in the broader benchmark use diagonal traces; select the transformation family supported by the current demos.

After a wrong answer, binary feedback rejects the whole candidate but gives no localization. Re-evaluate the rule itself against all demos rather than making repeated near-duplicate grids. Before serializing, validate the output dimensions, count every row's width, and ensure all cell values are valid integers 0–9.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/stripe-sequence-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/marker-ray-continuation.md", "content": "# Extending marker patterns as rays

When sparse input markers appear to encode a geometric trace, use demonstrations to infer its direction, step size, and colors rather than adding a one-cell continuation by intuition. Compare input and output coordinates: identify the seed points, infer the repeated displacement vector(s), and check the output for each predicted point. If the demo shows two rays or parallel traces, model both. Apply only the demonstrated continuation, stopping at the grid boundary and leaving unrelated cells unchanged.

A failed submission rejects the complete hypothesis but does not identify the bad cell. Revisit the full demo evidence and distinguish structural alternatives (for example, a single adjacent extension versus a repeated diagonal ray) before spending another attempt or requesting more examples.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/marker-ray-continuation.md"}
